# {{ company_prog_name }} — Operator Review

This skill answers one product question: **what genuinely needs the operator's
attention now?** Candidate feeds are evidence, not the answer. Run every read
below silently, make one explicit attention decision per candidate, then present
the result in **{{ operator_voice }}**.

The review is read-only. Do not draft, send, close, retire, suppress, write
memory, edit configuration, edit the repo, or commit. The one exception is the
engine catch-up in step 4, and it runs only after the operator explicitly says
yes to the one question there.

All commands run from the repo root with `.venv/bin/python -m cs …`.

{% include "desk-preamble.md.j2" %}

## 1. Load the operating boundary

Read `CLAUDE.md` in full. It owns identity, source authority, safety, and the
available read surfaces. In particular: Gmail owns existence/Sent dedup; the
engine owns its classifications, memory, and task ledger; the current
conversation owns what is true now.

## 2. Verify the live session and settings

Run:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs whoami
.venv/bin/python -m cs config
.venv/bin/python -m cs cron status --json
.venv/bin/python -m cs --version
git log -12 --date=short --format='%h %ad %s'
git status --porcelain
```

`cs config` is the only source for `cs_triage_mode` and the kill-switch state.
`cs --version` is the installed kernel, not the declared pin. `cron status` is
the only source for absent/paused/stale/ticking and the last run's timestamp and
outcome. Copy these values; do not derive them from logs or docs.

If `docs/owner-actions.md` exists, read only its open item headings. Otherwise
read the unresolved headings in `docs/active-context.md`. Treat either as the
operator's notes, not as live system state.

## 3. Check whether the engine has the newest mail

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs catchup --check
```

Ask one question — “Want me to run the catch-up now?” — only when that command
says catch-up is warranted, or the scheduled-run state is `absent` or `stale`.
Otherwise ask nothing.

## 4. Optional confirmed catch-up

If and only if the operator answered yes:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs catchup
```

Report its exact created/closed/updated task diff as a note. Do not retry a busy
answer. On no/no answer, skip once and continue.

## 5. Gather the complete candidate evidence

Run after the optional catch-up:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs review --json
.venv/bin/python -m cs unanswered --days 45 --crm --json --all-buckets
```

The first result contains drafts, open engine tasks with their ids/reasons/
actions/sources, human takeovers, out-of-band closures, campaign state, and the
last scheduled-run lines. The second contains all conversation buckets —
`open`, `resumed`, `automatic`, `courtesy`, `handled`, and `escalated` — plus
degradation notes. Do not replace either result with the human renderer.

{% if producer_adapter != 'none' %}
Also gather the producer candidates:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs plan -v --period 7d
```

Resolve every reachable candidate through its dossier before adding it to the
ledger:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs dossier <email>
```
{% endif %}

## 6. Build one candidate ledger

Create one internal row per distinct conversation, task, or draft. Give every
row a stable id:

- conversation: `thread:<thread_key>`;
- engine task without a matching conversation: `task:<full task id>`;
- draft without a matching conversation: `draft:uid-<uid>` or
  `draft:engine-<full id>`;
- producer-only candidate: `candidate:<stable producer id>`.

Merge provenance when two sources describe the same conversation. Never merge
two different `thread_key` values merely because the address is the same.
Attach the raw source facts, including task reason/action/timestamps, draft
handles/composed time/verdict, CRM evidence, handled/takeover records, and
degradation notes.

Include `open` and `resumed` conversations as attention candidates. Carry
`automatic`, `courtesy`, `handled`, and human-takeover rows as audit evidence so
they cannot disappear or be re-raised from memory.

## 7. Read the current conversation for every candidate

For every ledger row with an email address, run:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs thread <email> --json --full
```

Select the matching `thread_key` when the candidate has one. The command returns
search summaries enriched with the full chronological `emails` from
`emails.list_by_thread`. Read those messages, not only subject/snippet. Reuse one
read for several evidence rows only when their conversation identity actually
matches.

If the full read fails, the thread cannot be matched, or a phone-only task has
no current conversation surface, preserve the row and mark it `uncertain`.
Never promote missing evidence to work.

{% include "review-attention-contract.md.j2" %}

## 8. Present the operator's agenda

Write the report only after the completeness check in the shared contract
passes. Use this content shape and adapt the wording to
**{{ operator_voice }}**:

```text
{{ company_prog_name }} — attention review

Engine: {{ email_address }} <reachable state>
State: kernel <installed version> · sending: <on hold (your call)|active> · mode: <resolved mode>
Scheduled run: <configured|not configured> · last <timestamp or none> — <ran|skipped|none>
Evidence: <candidate count> candidates · <full-thread reads> current conversations · <degradation count> notes

Needs you now (<act_now count>):
| id | person / subject | concrete action | why now | sources |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| ... one row per act_now decision; include draft handles when present ... |

Needs judgement (<uncertain count>):
| id | person / subject | what could not be established | sources |
|---|---|---|---|
| ... omit the block when zero ... |

Not on today's agenda: waiting on others <n> · informational <n> · stale <n>
Stored-state disagreements:
- <task/draft id> — <stored claim> vs <current evidence> (<verdict>)
  ... only disagreements; omit when none ...

Taken over by a human: <rows with owner + age; omit when none>
Resolved outside email: <rows with date + reason; omit when none>
Campaign escalations: <named rows; plain outcomes stay counts; omit when none>

Repo: <concise git-log digest> (+ <uncommitted count>)
Waiting on you: <open note headings, max 5; omit when none>
Notes: <catch-up diff and degradations; omit when none>
```

The kill-switch appears once as neutral state and is never offered for removal.
Do not print raw candidate tables before or after the agenda. Do not call a
draft “ready to send”; call a current one “review this draft” and include its
handle. Do not call every engine task work; list a task only when its adjudicated
verdict is `act_now` or `uncertain`.

**End the review after the report.** Do not fix a sender list, close a task,
retire a draft, prepare a reply, or suggest changing configuration in this
turn. Wait for a new operator message that names the item and the action.
